{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JJUeh5DH7jzJ495L1E1BH2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tCITATION RULES — [@Key] & BibLaTeX OUTPUTS\n2\t\n3\tGoal\n4\t- Define how to represent citations in documents that use [@Key] syntax with a matching BibLaTeX database.\n5\t- Convert token citations from upstream workflows into standard [@Key] form and ensure a complete biblatex.bib when such output is requested.\n6\t\n7\t\n8\tCitation Representation (MANDATORY)\n9\t- When these rules apply, the final document MUST use `[@Key]` citation syntax only.\n10\t- Source tokens (from upstream workflows):\n11\t  - `[KB:filename.md]`\n12\t  - `[WEB:https://...]`\n13\t  - `[DOI:10.0000/xyz123]`\n14\t  - `[ARXIV:paper-id]`\n15\t  - `[ZOTERO:item_key]` (optional)\n16\t- During compilation:\n17\t  - CONVERT all supported tokens to `[@Key]` form.\n18\t  - Do NOT leave any token citations (`[KB:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]`) in the final document.\n19\t  - It is acceptable to keep raw URLs in prose **in addition to** [@Key] when the URL itself is content (e.g., pointing to an online tool), but the citation must still use [@Key].\n20\t\n21\tCitekey Rules\n22\t- Citekeys MUST follow the pattern: `^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]{2,64}$` (letters and digits only; start with a letter).\n23\t- Key format by number of authors:\n24\t  - 1 author: `AuthorYear` (e.g., `Kramer2015`)\n25\t  - 2 authors: `Author1Author2Year` (e.g., `WellsCoppersmith1994`, `AtkinsonBoore2006`)\n26\t  - 3+ authors: `FirstAuthorEtAlYear` (e.g., `BooreEtAl2014`, `AbrahamsonEtAl2016`)\n27\t  - Organization/committee: `OrganizationYear` (e.g., `SSHAC1997`, `ANCOLD2019`)\n28\t- For collisions (same key pattern): append a, b, c (e.g., `Boore2014a`, `Boore2014b`).\n29\t- Use only the surname portion of each author name (no initials, no given names).\n30\t- This convention is mandatory for cross-session consistency: the same paper must get the same key regardless of which session processes it.\n31\t\n32\tMapping Tokens → Keys\n33\t- For each token encountered in the selected content (including audit rationales when available):\n34\t  - Identify the underlying work (author/title/year/venue/url/doi/arxiv).\n35\t  - Assign or reuse a stable [@Key] according to the rules above.\n36\t  - Ensure that all mentions of the same work map to the **same** key.\n37\t- Maintain an internal mapping table such as `{\"TOKEN:original\": \"@Key\"}` during compilation.\n38\t\n39\tPlacement Rules\n40\t- After conversion, place `[@Key]` immediately after the sentence or clause it supports.\n41\t- Multiple keys are allowed, e.g. `... [@Kramer2015][@WellsCoppersmith1994]`.\n42\t- Avoid redundant repeats of the same key in a single sentence unless there is a clear structural reason.\n43\t- Never place `[@Key]` citations inside Markdown code blocks (fenced or indented).\n44\t- When converting token citations, do NOT convert tokens inside code blocks; treat them as literal code.\n45\t\n46\tDeduplication\n47\t- If multiple tokens refer to the same work, use a single `[@Key]` in the compiled text.\n48\t- If multiple paragraphs across sessions cite the same work, they should all reuse the same key; deduplicate entries in biblatex.bib, not in the prose.\n49\t\n50\tBibLaTeX Integration\n51\t- Use `rules/biblatex.md` as the authoritative contract for entry structure.\n52\t- For every distinct `[@Key]` in the compiled document:\n53\t  - Create exactly one BibLaTeX entry in `biblatex.bib`.\n54\t  - Populate fields such as: `author`, `title`, `year`, `journal`/`booktitle`/`publisher`, `volume`, `number`, `pages`, `doi`, `url`, `eprint` as applicable.\n55\t  - Choose an appropriate entry type: `@article`, `@inproceedings`, `@book`, `@online`, `@misc`, etc.\n56\t- Extract metadata from all available sources:\n57\t  - The original tokens (especially DOI/ARXIV/WEB identifiers).\n58\t  - Context captured in audit rationales or surrounding text.\n59\t  - Any MCP tools the workflow is allowed to call (e.g., Crossref/OpenAlex), if available.\n60\t\n61\tScope of These Rules\n62\t- These citation rules govern only **representation and bibliography** for documents that are already assembled (paragraph selection and evidence have been decided elsewhere).\n63\t- They do not assume or require knowledge of any specific workflow type; they apply whenever the orchestrator or task asks for `[@Key]` citations plus a `biblatex.bib` file.\n64\t- Evidence policies (when to fetch new sources, what counts as acceptable evidence, how to treat KB vs WEB) remain defined in the corresponding workflow files (e.g., `workflows/compile.md`, `workflows/audit.md`, `workflows/enhance.md`, `workflows/retrieve.md`).\n65\t\n66\tOutput Requirements\n67\t- Final documents (compile/audit/enhance/retrieve) MUST:\n68\t  - Use only `[@Key]` citations in the body text.\n69\t  - Contain no stray token citations.\n70\t  - Have a `biblatex.bib` file with exactly one entry per key used in the document.\n71\t  - Maintain a clear, consistent mapping between in-text [@Key] citations and BibLaTeX entries.\n72\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c9cf6565-2a41-4e2d-8f56-1d65c6100c5b","uuid":"452cf498-fa67-4d54-8d4c-5760ea6868fc","timestamp":"2026-03-31T00:38:38.813Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/citations.compile.md","content":"CITATION RULES — [@Key] & BibLaTeX OUTPUTS\n\nGoal\n- Define how to represent citations in documents that use [@Key] syntax with a matching BibLaTeX database.\n- Convert token citations from upstream workflows into standard [@Key] form and ensure a complete biblatex.bib when such output is requested.\n\n\nCitation Representation (MANDATORY)\n- When these rules apply, the final document MUST use `[@Key]` citation syntax only.\n- Source tokens (from upstream workflows):\n  - `[KB:filename.md]`\n  - `[WEB:https://...]`\n  - `[DOI:10.0000/xyz123]`\n  - `[ARXIV:paper-id]`\n  - `[ZOTERO:item_key]` (optional)\n- During compilation:\n  - CONVERT all supported tokens to `[@Key]` form.\n  - Do NOT leave any token citations (`[KB:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]`) in the final document.\n  - It is acceptable to keep raw URLs in prose **in addition to** [@Key] when the URL itself is content (e.g., pointing to an online tool), but the citation must still use [@Key].\n\nCitekey Rules\n- Citekeys MUST follow the pattern: `^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]{2,64}$` (letters and digits only; start with a letter).\n- Key format by number of authors:\n  - 1 author: `AuthorYear` (e.g., `Kramer2015`)\n  - 2 authors: `Author1Author2Year` (e.g., `WellsCoppersmith1994`, `AtkinsonBoore2006`)\n  - 3+ authors: `FirstAuthorEtAlYear` (e.g., `BooreEtAl2014`, `AbrahamsonEtAl2016`)\n  - Organization/committee: `OrganizationYear` (e.g., `SSHAC1997`, `ANCOLD2019`)\n- For collisions (same key pattern): append a, b, c (e.g., `Boore2014a`, `Boore2014b`).\n- Use only the surname portion of each author name (no initials, no given names).\n- This convention is mandatory for cross-session consistency: the same paper must get the same key regardless of which session processes it.\n\nMapping Tokens → Keys\n- For each token encountered in the selected content (including audit rationales when available):\n  - Identify the underlying work (author/title/year/venue/url/doi/arxiv).\n  - Assign or reuse a stable [@Key] according to the rules above.\n  - Ensure that all mentions of the same work map to the **same** key.\n- Maintain an internal mapping table such as `{\"TOKEN:original\": \"@Key\"}` during compilation.\n\nPlacement Rules\n- After conversion, place `[@Key]` immediately after the sentence or clause it supports.\n- Multiple keys are allowed, e.g. `... [@Kramer2015][@WellsCoppersmith1994]`.\n- Avoid redundant repeats of the same key in a single sentence unless there is a clear structural reason.\n- Never place `[@Key]` citations inside Markdown code blocks (fenced or indented).\n- When converting token citations, do NOT convert tokens inside code blocks; treat them as literal code.\n\nDeduplication\n- If multiple tokens refer to the same work, use a single `[@Key]` in the compiled text.\n- If multiple paragraphs across sessions cite the same work, they should all reuse the same key; deduplicate entries in biblatex.bib, not in the prose.\n\nBibLaTeX Integration\n- Use `rules/biblatex.md` as the authoritative contract for entry structure.\n- For every distinct `[@Key]` in the compiled document:\n  - Create exactly one BibLaTeX entry in `biblatex.bib`.\n  - Populate fields such as: `author`, `title`, `year`, `journal`/`booktitle`/`publisher`, `volume`, `number`, `pages`, `doi`, `url`, `eprint` as applicable.\n  - Choose an appropriate entry type: `@article`, `@inproceedings`, `@book`, `@online`, `@misc`, etc.\n- Extract metadata from all available sources:\n  - The original tokens (especially DOI/ARXIV/WEB identifiers).\n  - Context captured in audit rationales or surrounding text.\n  - Any MCP tools the workflow is allowed to call (e.g., Crossref/OpenAlex), if available.\n\nScope of These Rules\n- These citation rules govern only **representation and bibliography** for documents that are already assembled (paragraph selection and evidence have been decided elsewhere).\n- They do not assume or require knowledge of any specific workflow type; they apply whenever the orchestrator or task asks for `[@Key]` citations plus a `biblatex.bib` file.\n- Evidence policies (when to fetch new sources, what counts as acceptable evidence, how to treat KB vs WEB) remain defined in the corresponding workflow files (e.g., `workflows/compile.md`, `workflows/audit.md`, `workflows/enhance.md`, `workflows/retrieve.md`).\n\nOutput Requirements\n- Final documents (compile/audit/enhance/retrieve) MUST:\n  - Use only `[@Key]` citations in the body text.\n  - Contain no stray token citations.\n  - Have a `biblatex.bib` file with exactly one entry per key used in the document.\n  - Maintain a clear, consistent mapping between in-text [@Key] citations and BibLaTeX entries.\n","numLines":72,"startLine":1,"totalLines":72}}}
